# Every conversion the corpus files, as an edge between two units. One row per fusion part that
# carries a factor: the unit it converts from, the unit it converts to, and the three bounds.
#
# The endpoints are filed facts
#   Nothing here parses a unit string. A factor's own unit reads `GPU-hour per GPU` and only the
#   denominator half of that is structural, so reading the pair off the label would mean
#   splitting free text on a preposition, which is the `LIKE '% per %'` this directory has
#   removed twice. It is unnecessary: a factor converts a part layer into its composed layer, and
#   both of those layers file their own unit. The edge is the two layers, and the label is only
#   a label.
#
# A crossing with no factor at all
#   An edge can cross a unit boundary with no factor at all, and finding those is what this
#   relation is for. `pessoas` to `people`, and `turnos por semana` to `shifts per week`, are
#   three such crossings in this corpus. A composition like that asserts that a Portuguese unit
#   and an English one are the same thing, and asserts it by omitting the element, which
#   `composition/converted.sqlc` reads as a factor of one. Two editions of one word is a
#   judgement this repository has twice refused to make in SQL, and an omission lets it arrive
#   as an absence instead.
#
# How the crossings are filed
#   Two file `[1, 1, 1]` with a `boundOrigin` and a provenance, so the assertion has an author
#   and a note saying what would make it false; the third files `unmeasured`, which is a
#   composer saying they looked at a real crossing and could not size it.
#   `checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor` is what keeps it that way, and it is why the count
#   of factorless crossings is zero rather than three, and not by luck.
#
# An omission means nothing to convert
#   The parts that still omit the element are all correct: their unit and their composed
#   layer's already agree, so the identity is forced by structure the document states and there
#   is no author to name. That is the whole distinction the rule enforces: an omission is only
#   ever allowed to mean "nothing to convert".
#
# A typed-absent factor is not an edge either
#   For a different reason: `unmeasured` says nobody sized the conversion, so there is no number
#   to put on the arrow.
#
# The `quantity = 'nameplate'` pin is a slice
#   It is declared here because it does not show in the key. A unit is a property of a claim,
#   not of a layer: `pm.claim.unit` is per position, and arithmetic/remainder.sqlc carries a
#   `not comparable` verdict for exactly the layer whose demand and nameplate disagree. So
#   reading the edge off one quantity is a σπ, and the aggregate over all three would be a
#   different relation. It is sound while no layer files two units across its quantities.
#
# Which is a claim about the corpus, so it is a law and not a sentence
#   algebra/layer_units.sqlc holds it: `|π(layer, unit)| = |π(layer)|` over layers/quantities.sqlc,
#   run every time the laws run. Before that it was this header saying to run a query, and a
#   sentence telling a reader to run a query is not a program that runs it. The day a layer files
#   two units, this graph and checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close are reading a fifth of the
#   evidence and saying nothing about the rest, and the law is what says so.
#
# The word `nameplate` is checked now
#   `quantity` is `public.layer_quantity`, so the pin below is a value of a declared type and a
#   misspelling fails when this query is parsed. Spelled as free text it matched nothing, and a
#   conversion graph with no edges is not obviously wrong to look at.
-- asrt:Part/asrt:factor at the nameplate, as part-layer-unit to composed-layer-unit.
SELECT DISTINCT
       p.part_unit     AS from_unit,
       p.composed_unit AS to_unit,
       p.factor_low, p.factor_mode, p.factor_high,
       p.composition AS filing, p.composed_layer AS layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/part_quantities.sqlc)
) p
WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate'
  AND p.factor_state = 'stated'
